## Condition (Co)

Evaluate ONE rule against ONE subject and say what was seen. Returns the status, the observed value, and one sentence of reasoning.

```ts
import ConditionCommand from '@cardstack/catalog/cards/audit/commands/condition-command';

let { status, observed, reason } = await new ConditionCommand(
  commandContext,
).execute({
  rule,                 // a ValidationRuleField
  subject,              // any card
  hasEvidence: false,   // the caller knows; the engine cannot see attachments
} as any);
// status.status === 'unproven', reason: '…allowed. No evidence attached.'
```

**Pure — it writes nothing, anywhere.** That is what makes it safe to call from a preview, a badge, a report and the Audit command without any of them worrying about side effects, and it is why a verdict is always recomputed rather than cached: a rule's answer is only true as of the data it just read.

It re-fetches the subject before reading it. A rule may point through a link the caller never loaded, and an unloaded link reads exactly like an empty one — which would turn a loading state into a compliance failure.

`hasEvidence` is the caller's business: the engine cannot see attachments, so a rule marked `evidenceRequired` that would otherwise pass returns `unproven`. That distinction is why the status vocabulary has six values and not two.

## Example
The linked example is a lapsed retainer. Rule CON-02 from `AuditorBot/contract-controls` against it returns `fail`, as the recorded audit result for it does: its end date has passed.

## Non-goals
Does not write a result, raise a finding, or stamp anything. Does not run a rule-set — that is Policy. Does not decide severity: the rule carries the severity a failure starts at.
